input.question:
Research state-of-the-art and novel techniques for a fast batched square Householder QR factorization on NVIDIA B200 (sm_100, Blackwell, TF32/tf32x3 tensor cores) as custom Triton/CuTe kernels. The output contract requires a packed (H, tau) flat global Householder representation passing FP64 checks (||R - Q^T A||, ||Q^T Q - I||, tril(Q^T A,-1)~0) over ill-conditioned, rank-deficient, near-collinear, banded, and row-scaled inputs. The bottleneck is the panel factorization (tall-skinny n x IB QR serial reflector dependency chain, ~45% of runtime, critical path), especially at grid-starved shapes (batch=60/n=1024 = 60 blocks on 148 SMs, batch=8/n=2048, batch=2/n=4096) and the GPU-saturating batch=640/n=512. Need: (1) Householder reconstruction from TSQR (LAPACK xORHR_COL/xUNHR_COL, Ballard-Demmel-Grigori-Hoemmen-Knight 2014/2015) made cheap and GEMM-heavy; (2) TSQR/CAQR tree panel factorization mapped to one-CTA-per-row-block without device-wide barriers, combined with ORHR_COL to emit flat (H,tau); (3) panel critical-path shortening (UT-transform, recursive geqrt3, look-ahead, 2023-2026 GPU techniques); (4) batched QR (MAGMA, cuSOLVER, recent tensor-core batched-QR papers) and saturating-vs-starved regime handling; (5) mixed-precision robust QR (tf32x3/3xTF32 panel, iterative refinement/re-orthogonalization keeping FP32 flat-(H,tau) accuracy); (6) a ranked list of distinct buildable approaches including novel combinations.</question>
